  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a broad trough extends from the Dakotas to New \nEngland. A weak ridge is over the extreme Southeast (FL/GA/SC). The \nlatest surface analysis shows a slow moving cold front just upstream \nover Middle Tennessee. Scattered showers will continue overnight \nmainly north of I-40. A few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out \nbut showers have been shallow so far. This afternoon scattered \nshowers and isolated storms will be mainly south of I-40 and east of \nKnoxville, closer to the foothills and mountains as the slow moving \ncold front moves into East Tennessee. Rain chances are highest on \nSunday as a weak shortwave moves through the Ohio Valley to the \nnorth. The frontal boundary will be nearly stationary just to our \nsouth bringing overrunning conditions. Additional rainfall this \nweekend will be 1 to 2 inches with isolated higher amounts. Some \nlocations already received 1 to 2 inches over the past several\nhours due to repeated showers. \n\nThe rain will end by Monday morning as a trough pushes the cold \nfront out of the region. At the surface, high pressure settles in \nover the Eastern U.S. Dry weather is expected all next week with \nhigh pressure dominating the pattern. Temperatures will be near \nnormal early in the week with a slight warming trend late in the \nweek.",
